ggml-rpc: let an embedding host register servers and stop its own server safely

An embedding host (here: the java-llama.cpp JNI binding, which runs llama.cpp inside a
JVM) cannot use ggml-rpc as it is:

1. Every client-side connection problem is a GGML_ABORT: a malformed endpoint, a server
   that is not running, a failed handshake. For a standalone tool that is an error exit;
   inside a JVM it kills the whole application because of a typo in "--rpc". The
   registration path now reports these as failures instead:
   - rpc_dispatcher::start() returns false;
   - try_get_dispatcher() returns nullptr;
   - ggml_backend_rpc_get_device_count() returns 0;
   - ggml_backend_rpc_add_server() returns nullptr.
   add_rpc_devices() (common/arg.cpp) now throws std::invalid_argument naming the server,
   where it used to register nullptr and silently run without the requested server.

   Every path that runs AFTER registration (buffer/graph/tensor calls) keeps the original
   contract through get_dispatcher(), which still aborts. A server that vanishes mid-run
   stays fatal; that needs a real error path through the backend interface.

2. add_server() returned a cached registration without checking that the server is still
   there, so re-using an endpoint whose server had stopped passed registration and aborted
   on the first tensor upload. The cache hit is now re-checked.

   ggml_backend_rpc_get_device_memory() of a server that went away reports 0/0 instead of
   aborting. Registered devices stay in ggml's process-wide registry forever, and code that
   never uses them still queries their memory: common_init() lists every device at -lv 4,
   and --list-devices does too. 0/0 is what --fit already reads as an unknown budget.
   ggml_backend_rpc_init() keeps aborting if the registration fails.

3. ggml_backend_rpc_start_server() cannot be stopped: while(true) around accept(). Two new
   entry points, both also exposed through get_proc_address:
   - ggml_backend_rpc_stop_server() disconnects the client and every live peer socket.
     Listener accept and outgoing peer connect poll cancellation at 50 ms intervals; no
     wake-up connection can fill the listen backlog and block the stopping thread. The
     loop releases its backends when it ends.
   - ggml_backend_rpc_server_listening() tells the host whether the socket was bound.
   The transport stays initialised on the way out, because WSACleanup() would invalidate
   every other RPC socket in the process.

4. transport: no SIGPIPE when the peer went away (MSG_NOSIGNAL / SO_NOSIGPIPE), a
   socket_t::shutdown() (on Windows also CancelIoEx: shutdown alone does not wake a recv
   blocked in another thread there), and the file descriptors that connect()/create_server()/accept()
   leaked on their error paths are closed.

Upstream-submittable; not filed upstream yet. The runnable guard is
src/test/cpp/test_rpc.cpp: it links both new functions and exercises the registration and
stop paths over loopback. test_rpc_comm.cpp drives protocol-v8 COMM_INIT through the C
API: loopback/network binding, missing peer, stalled caps handshakes in both ranks,
normal peer setup, socket cleanup and restart. Subprocess deadlines bound failures.

5. COMM_INIT preserves the configured server bind address instead of opening a wildcard
   listener for a loopback-only server. Peer sockets are weakly registered before any
   caps I/O, under the same mutex as stop; stop also catches a peer accepted/connected
   concurrently. Normal COMM_FREE/disconnect still frees them immediately. The rank-1
   retry loop checks cancellation too. The former main-listener wake-up connection is
   removed: repeated C-API stops while the peer was absent filled its backlog.

Drop-check on each upstream bump: inspect comm_init, the stoppable accept/connect
paths and stop_server. Drop upstream-fixed hunks instead of mechanically refreshing them.

Refreshed at b12079 (#28967, NCCL for multi-node tensor parallelism): COMM_INIT accepts
world-1 peers on rank 0 instead of one (comm_state::peers, a vector in rank order), factors
the capability exchange into comm_caps_handshake(), has every peer send its rank after it, and
negotiates the communicator over those sockets (comm_bootstrap_exchange) before it answers.
The cancellation contract is carried over unchanged: every accept of the loop and the
connect/retry of a higher rank observe the stop flag, and each peer socket is registered with
rpc_server_track_peer() before its first blocking read, so a stop shuts it down under the
mutex wherever COMM_INIT blocks on it -- the handshake, the rank, or the bootstrap exchange.
Not covered: a backend communicator's own init (ggml_backend_comm_init_rank, CUDA with NCCL)
blocks inside the backend until every rank has joined and has no cancellation.

Refreshed at b12217 (#29413, "RPC: Improve loading times", protocol 9.0.0): the HELLO answer
carries server feature flags and negotiate_hello() takes the dispatcher's flags to fill; the
hunk that turns a failed handshake of rpc_dispatcher::start() into a reported failure is
rewritten over that call. Nothing else moved.

Reduced at b12283 (#30415, "ggml-rpc : fail gracefully on unreachable endpoints and failed
handshakes"): upstream took the client half -- rpc_dispatcher::start() returns false instead of
aborting, negotiate_hello() reports a failed exchange, get_dispatcher() returns nullptr and does
not cache a failed attempt, and every consumer answers nullptr conservatively (device memory
0/0, device count 0). Those hunks are dropped, with this patch's try_get_dispatcher() /
aborting get_dispatcher() pair. What stays of the client: an endpoint registered earlier is
re-checked on the next ggml_backend_rpc_add_server() (upstream returns the cached registration
of a server that has gone away), and ggml_backend_rpc_init() aborts by name when the
registration of a reachable server fails. The server half (stop, listening, COMM_INIT bind
address and cancellation, transport) is unchanged.

